Reminder: Singleton Design Pattern

1) Make the constructor private so that objects of the class cannot be created directly
from outside the class.

2) Create a method to return the object
Since the constructor is private, we cannot create an object using the new keyword from
outside the class. We need a method that creates and returns the object.
This method should be static, because we need to access it without creating an object first.
A normal (non-static) method cannot be called without an object.


3) Create a static variable to store the object
Create a variable to hold the single instance of the class.
This variable should also be static, because the static method needs to access it.
Inside the static method, check whether the variable is null.
If it is null, create a new object and assign it to the variable.
Then return the object through the method.
If the variable is not null, simply return the existing object.

